================================================================================
DEPENDENCY LICENCES
================================================================================

  libm  MIT OR Apache-2.0 — a pure-Rust port of musl's libm. Permissive into
        GPL-3.0. Required, not optional: in no_std the f64 transcendentals are
        std-only, and GSL calls them throughout.

  uom   Apache-2.0 OR MIT — dimensional analysis. Permissive into GPL-3.0.
        OPTIONAL, behind the default-on `transfer-fn` feature, and used only by
        the transfer-function blocks, where a sample time genuinely is a Time
        and a signal genuinely is a Ratio. Every numerics layer takes bare f64.
        Built with default-features off: uom's defaults include `std`.

================================================================================
PRIOR ART IN RUST — ACKNOWLEDGEMENT
================================================================================

  PETIR is not the first attempt to bring these algorithms to Rust, and the
  people who got there earlier deserve saying so by name. Two crates in
  particular cover ground PETIR covers, and both are in this workspace's
  dependency graph already (root `[workspace.dependencies]`):

    peroxide 0.41.2   MIT OR Apache-2.0
                      Tae Geun Kim ("axect", <axect@outlook.kr>)
                      https://github.com/Axect/Peroxide
                      A full numeric/scientific-computing stack for Rust:
                      linear algebra, statistics, ODE integration,
                      interpolation, root finding, special functions,
                      automatic differentiation.

    roots 0.0.8       BSD-2-Clause
                      Mikhail Vorotilov <mikhail.vorotilov@gmail.com>
                      https://github.com/vorot/roots
                      Closed-form solvers for polynomials up to quartic, and
                      the bracketed iterative root finders (bisection, regula
                      falsi, Brent-Dekker, inverse quadratic, Newton-Raphson).

  WHERE THEY OVERLAP PETIR, MODULE BY MODULE. Recorded specifically rather
  than as a generic wave at "scientific computing", because a vague
  acknowledgement is worth less than an accurate one. Read from the published
  sources at the versions above on 2026-09-15, not from memory:

    petir module            peroxide 0.41.2              roots 0.0.8
    --------------------    -------------------------    ---------------------
    roots (6 methods)       numerical/root.rs,           numerical/{brent,
                            numerical/newton.rs          regula_falsi, secant,
                                                         newton_raphson,
                                                         inverse_quadratic}.rs
    poly (exact solvers)    structure/polynomial.rs      analytical/{linear,
                                                         quadratic, cubic,
                                                         quartic, biquadratic}
    linalg::qr              traits/matrix.rs `QR<M>`     --
    interp (spline)         numerical/{interp,spline}    --
    integration             numerical/integral.rs        --
    ode                     numerical/ode.rs             --
    min                     numerical/optimize.rs        --
    cheb                    structure/polynomial.rs      --
                            `chebyshev_polynomial`,
                            numerical/interp.rs
                            `chebyshev_nodes`

  THE NEWEST WORK IN PETIR IS THE MOST OVERLAPPED, WHICH IS WORTH SAYING
  PLAINLY. The QR factorisation, the least-squares path and the Chebyshev
  machinery added in September 2026 are precisely the ground peroxide had
  already covered -- it carries a `QR<M>` decomposition, `chebyshev_polynomial`
  and `chebyshev_nodes`. Arriving at the same place later, by a different
  route, is not discovery.

  ONE PRECISION, so this does not overstate their reach: peroxide's
  `least_square` (src/ml/reg.rs) is simple linear regression -- it fits
  `ax + b` from accumulated means -- not a general least-squares fit in an
  arbitrary basis, which is what `ChebSeries::fit` does. peroxide has the QR
  that such a fit needs; this file does not claim it exposes the fit itself.

  AND WHERE THEY GO FURTHER THAN PETIR, which matters just as much:

    - roots solves QUARTIC and BIQUADRATIC polynomials in closed form.
      PETIR stops at the cubic (`poly/{linear,quadratic,cubic}_eqn.rs`) and
      has no quartic solver at all. If you need one, that crate has it and
      this one does not.
    - peroxide carries eigenvalue decomposition, sparse matrices, automatic
      differentiation, a dataframe and a statistics layer. PETIR has none of
      these and no plan to acquire them.

  TO BE PRECISE ABOUT WHAT THIS IS AND IS NOT. This is an acknowledgement of
  prior art, NOT a statement of derivation. **No code in PETIR is copied from,
  translated from, or derived from either crate.** Every routine here traces to
  the upstream named in its own file header -- GSL, ARM optimized-routines,
  OpenFOAM by way of outram-foam-basic-lib, or the GNU Octave control package
  by way of chem-eng -- and `tests/verbatim_provenance.rs` and the code-to-code
  reference sets under `reference-data/` are what hold that claim to account.
  Neither peroxide nor roots is a dependency of PETIR, and no licence
  obligation to either arises from this crate.

  It is recorded because credit for solving a problem first is not contingent
  on someone else having used your solution, and because a reader comparing
  PETIR against the Rust ecosystem should be pointed at the work that came
  before it rather than left to assume there was none.

  Both crates were EARLY to this in Rust -- doing the unglamorous work of
  getting real numerical methods into the language while the ecosystem was
  still thin, without which "just use the Rust crate" would not have been a
  sentence anyone could say. That is the part worth acknowledging, and it is
  not diminished by PETIR having different constraints.

  WHY PETIR EXISTS ALONGSIDE THEM, since that is the obvious next question:
  PETIR is `no_std` unconditionally and carries provenance to a specific
  upstream commit for every routine, because this workspace needs kernels that
  build for bare metal and wasm AND that a reviewer can diff line-for-line
  against a reference implementation with its own test suite. Those are
  constraints particular to this project, not deficiencies in either crate
  above.

